<Project>
    <!--
      The version is written in exactly one tracked file, build.yaml, because that
      is the file the plugin package carries to a server and to a catalogue. Every
      assembly version below is read back out of it, so the packaged version and
      the compiled version cannot drift apart.

      Two matches rather than one capture group: MSBuild converts the returned
      Match to its string, so the first pulls out the version line and the second
      pulls the number out of that line.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <!-- The graph is watched, not only recorded. NuGet checks the restored graph
             against the advisory database and NU1901 to NU1904 are raised to errors, so a
             known-vulnerable package fails the restore. Left as warnings they would be
             true and green, printed into a log nobody reads.
    
             This board committed a lockfile and ran no audit, which is the worse half of
             the pair: the graph was held still and nothing looked at it. Eleven boards of
             this fleet were in that state on 2026-08-18, and the twelfth is why it
             matters. jellyfin-plugin-watchlist was told by Scorecard about one high
             advisory; switching this on found a second one rated critical that Scorecard
             had never reported.
    
             Mode all rather than direct: both of those arrived under the Jellyfin
             packages and neither was referenced by a project file, so a direct-only audit
             would have found neither.
    
             Nothing is pinned here. Measured with the audit forced on from the command
             line, this board reports no advisory today; the entry is the detector, not a
             repair.
    
             Note for whoever edits this comment: an XML comment may not contain two
             hyphens in a row. A draft elsewhere in this fleet spelled out a command line
             flag, left the file malformed, and the build failed on a property defined
             twenty lines below. -->
        <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
        <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
        <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
        <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <PropertyGroup>
        <PluginManifestPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)build.yaml</PluginManifestPath>
        <PluginManifestText>$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($(PluginManifestPath)))</PluginManifestText>
        <PluginManifestVersionLine>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match($(PluginManifestText), '(?m)^version:[ ]*"[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+"'))</PluginManifestVersionLine>
        <PluginManifestVersion>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::Match($(PluginManifestVersionLine), '[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+'))</PluginManifestVersion>
        <Version>$(PluginManifestVersion)</Version>
        <AssemblyVersion>$(PluginManifestVersion)</AssemblyVersion>
        <FileVersion>$(PluginManifestVersion)</FileVersion>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      The oldest server release in each supported line, as the package version
      that server ships. A plugin compiled against a later release of a line can
      call something that release added, and it then fails to load for everybody
      still on the floor of that line. Compiling against the floor is what stops
      that, so these are the versions every project here references: the plugin,
      which ships, and the suite, which carries the same assemblies because there
      is no server under it.

      Each floor is written here once and nowhere else. Two projects referenced
      the same two versions before this, so a bump was two edits that could be
      made one at a time, and the ABI floor jobs in .github/workflows/build.yaml
      refuse a project that names a version of its own instead of reading these.

      They sit beside the manifest reader above because the manifest is the other
      half of the same statement: build.yaml declares targetAbi 10.11.0.0 to the
      server, and JellyfinFloorNet9 is the package version that claim is only
      true if the plugin was built against. The floor job compares the two.

      The 12.0 line has published no stable release. Its floor is the oldest
      release candidate that carries a net10.0 target, and it moves to the
      release when there is one:

          curl -s https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/jellyfin.controller/index.json
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <JellyfinFloorNet9>10.11.0</JellyfinFloorNet9>
        <JellyfinFloorNet10>12.0.0-rc1</JellyfinFloorNet10>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      The SQLite stack is the server's too, and these are its floors, read the
      same way and for the same reason as the two above.

      Nothing of it travels in the package. The plugin project is a library, so
      the assemblies of its package references are not copied to the build
      output at all, and the output is what the packaging tool zips: the archive
      holds meta.json and one assembly. So whichever version the reference names
      is the version the assembly binds, and the server has to supply that one
      or a later one on the same major.

      A strong-named assembly found BELOW the referenced version is not found,
      and the failure is silent until the store is first opened. Built against
      9.0.11, the plugin logged a FileNotFoundException on a 10.11.0 server,
      created no data folder and stored no play, while build.yaml went on
      offering the archive to that server. Issue #330.

      What each supported server ships, read out of the image rather than out of
      a release note:

          cid=$(docker create jellyfin/jellyfin:10.11.0)
          docker cp "$cid:/jellyfin/Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.dll" .
          docker rm "$cid"
          [System.Reflection.AssemblyName]::GetAssemblyName("Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.dll").Version

          jellyfin/jellyfin:10.11.0     9.0.10.0
          jellyfin/jellyfin:10.11.11    9.0.11.0
          jellyfin/jellyfin:12.0-rc1    10.0.9.0
          jellyfin/jellyfin:12.0-rc4    10.0.10.0

      The floor of a line is the oldest of those, so every later server on the
      line satisfies the reference. The package version and the assembly version
      agree part for part here, which is what lets the suite compare a package
      floor of 9.0.10 against an assembly reference of 9.0.10.0.

      These move when a line's floor server moves, which is the same moment the
      Jellyfin floor above moves, and the ABI floor jobs in
      .github/workflows/build.yaml refuse a project file that names a version of
      its own instead of reading these.
    -->
    <PropertyGroup>
        <SqliteFloorNet9>9.0.10</SqliteFloorNet9>
        <SqliteFloorNet10>10.0.9</SqliteFloorNet10>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      A version that could not be read leaves these properties empty, and the SDK
      then substitutes its own default without saying so. That was measured while
      writing this: a three-part version in build.yaml produced a build that
      succeeded and stamped the SDK default. Refuse instead, so a malformed or
      moved version line stops the build at the file that holds it.

      The condition reads PluginManifestVersion and not Version, because the SDK
      has already filled Version in by the time any target runs, so a guard on
      Version can never fire. That was the first shape of this guard and it
      passed the regression it was written for.
    -->
    <Target Name="RefuseAVersionThatCouldNotBeReadFromTheManifest"
            BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild">
        <Error Condition="'$(PluginManifestVersion)' == ''"
               Text="No four-part version line was found in $(PluginManifestPath). That file is the one place the version is written, and Directory.Build.props reads it from there." />
    </Target>
</Project>
